# --- python ---
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# --- SECRETS: never commit any of these ---
# Live session cookies, provider keys, local config with inline secrets.
#
# ``config.toml`` is in this list and that is the sharpest edge in the file. The
# operator's config is *meant* to sit in the repo -- they copy it from
# config.example.toml, which is tracked, and it belongs next to the docs they
# are following -- and it holds a live ``sessionid``. So the one credential
# location this project actively invites someone to create is the one a rule
# scoped to artifacts/ can never cover. Matched by exact name, so the example
# stays tracked.
config.toml
config.local.toml
.env
.env.*
*.env
secrets/
credentials/
*.sessionid

# --- RUNTIME STATE: checkpoints, artifacts, traces ---
# The tool defaults its data directory OUTSIDE the working tree, and
# support.paths.assert_outside_repo() *refuses* a data_dir inside it -- it
# raises, it does not warn. So these rules are not the guard. They exist for the
# one case the guard cannot cover: someone running the tool with a data
# directory on another machine, or before the check existed, or with a stray copy
# of an artifact dropped in by hand.
#
# WHICH MEANS THEY MUST BE SCOPED BY PATH. They used to end with `*.png` and
# `*.html`, and that is where the golden DOM corpus went missing.
#
# A bare extension rule has no scope. It matches every file of that name
# everywhere, including the seven committed HTML fixtures the anchor-drift suite
# reads and asserts against -- which were therefore invisible to git, untracked,
# and not even reported as untracked. All 27 of the tests that read them passed
# on the machine that wrote them and failed on a fresh clone, which is the exact
# failure this repository is about: a claim in a docstring ("a fixture is
# committed to git") that the repository did not support.
#
# The specific extension rules are gone rather than negated. Negation would also
# work, but it leaves a trap for the next person who adds a broad rule, and the
# real protection is that a data directory inside the repo cannot be configured
# in the first place. tests/test_repository_hygiene.py enforces both halves: the
# fixtures are tracked, and no broad rule can swallow them again.
state/
artifacts/
traces/
checkpoints/
runs/
*.checkpoint.jsonl
*.trace.zip

# --- reference repo scratch (not part of this build) ---
/vendor/
/tmp/
